5.1.m:
Sūta said:
Great is this burden of a question which has been uttered by you;
nevertheless, having bowed to the self-manifest one, I shall proclaim it.

5.5.m:
Therefore, taking refuge in the forest, I shall now perform austerity;
I will not again govern the kingdom, which has been entrusted to my son.”
Thus ends the fifth chapter, titled “Viśvāmitra’s Performance of the Twelve-Year Sacrifice for Triśaṅku to Ascend to Heaven in Bodily Form,” in the story of Triśaṅku, in the glorification of the Hāṭakeśvara sacred field, in the Nāgarakhaṇḍa, the sixth [book] in the eighty-one-thousand-verse collection of the glorious Skanda Mahāpurāṇa.

5.6.m:
The Blessed Lord said:
“I am well pleased, may it be well with you! Request a boon, O good sage;
whatever is constantly held in your heart, I shall grant it all without doubt.”

5.8.m:
Brahmā said:
That Dānava was slain by you, O Śakra, with that thunderbolt;
the sinful one has gone under the power of death; you should not be afraid. 104

Indra said:
Many Dānavas indeed were slain by me previously;
the murder of a brahmin did not arise; how is there murder for me now? 106

Brahmā said:
They were slain by you in battle according to the duty of a kṣatriya, O Vāsava;
all those Dānavas were pure; therefore no sin arose. 107
